562 0 obj
<<
/Pg 47 0 R
/P 1879 0 R
/A 1880 0 R
/Alt (Six-panel line graph showing how financial conditions in the United States and Australia respond over 24 months to a 1 percentage point rise in each country's 10-year yield, where the rise is driven by higher US term premia. In the United States, mortgage rates rise by about 1 percentage point straight away and stay there. Over two years, house prices fall by around 7 per cent and housing credit falls by around 2 per cent. In Australia, mortgage rates barely move in the first year and rise later by about 0.5 percentage points, while house prices and housing credit change little. Corporate bond yields rise in both countries, and by more in Australia. Share prices fall by around 5 to 10 per cent in both countries. The US dollar appreciates, while the Australian dollar depreciates. Most of the Australian estimates are imprecise, with wide confidence intervals that include zero.)
/K 6
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
>>
endobj
